        id: "f82-tint-metal-fresnel",
        state: "CLOSED",
        note: "DONE at v4584. physics/render/fresnelF82.mjs adds f82Tint(cosI, F0, b): the published Hoffman/Adobe-Standard-Material correction to Schlick's Fresnel, a term shaped mu(1-mu)^6 that is exactly zero at mu=0 and mu=1 (so it cannot disturb the two endpoints Schlick already gets right) and scaled to hit exactly b -- the caller's own edge-tint colour -- at mu=1/7 (roughly 82 degrees, what \"F82\" names). *** NOT VENDORED: portsmouth/F82-tint-generator (this entry's own prior note) was never read. *** The formula was RE-DERIVED from its three defining constraints and cross-checked against the exact published constants (17.6513846, and 49/6) boksajak/brdf's MIT-adjacent evalFresnelHoffman() ships -- read only to verify the derivation's numbers, nothing copied. A genuine, independent, computed finding along the way: within the mu(1-mu)^n family, the bump's OWN peak sits at mu=1/(n+1), so n=6 is not an arbitrary literature constant alongside \"pin at 1/7\" -- it is the unique exponent that puts the correction's maximum leverage exactly where the curve is pinned, verified numerically in the gate rather than argued from calculus alone. Graded the way this entry's own `how` asked: against a number the tree already had, not a tolerance -- at F0=1, b=1 (where f82Tint collapses to \"Fresnel is identically 1\", the same degenerate case split-sum-selfcheck.mjs's own anchor uses), f82Tint's directional albedo through microfacet.mjs's directionalAlbedoSplit(alpha, cosO, Fof) hook agrees with physics/render/energyCompensation.mjs's table to the same order split-sum's own anchor measures (worst 3.53e-3 across 12 (alpha,cosO) points), and a genuinely tinted case is confirmed NOT to collapse to that same table (the negative control). physics/render/fresnelF82Wgsl.mjs is the hand-written WGSL twin (splitSumWgsl.mjs's convention, not microfacetWgsl.mjs's GLSL-translation one -- no GLSL sibling exists), graded against the CPU function on the native Dawn/SwiftShader backend (worst 2.5e-7, f32 floor) with a sign-flip sabotage caught (3.4e-1 disagreement) and confirmed BLIND at the two endpoints alone (which is why the gate samples the interior, not only F(0) and F(1)); tools/ship/crossBackend-selfcheck.mjs's own corpus now runs it on both backends too (33/33 identical), the one symbol the census found unaccounted, registered the same round it shipped. Wired into physics/render/principled.mjs's specular Fresnel term (evaluated at cosM, same angle schlick() already used there) as an opt-in `edgeTint` parameter -- omitting it, or setting it to exactly what Schlick already gives at the pin, reproduces the untinted BSDF bit for bit through the FULL composition (0.00e+0 across 54 combinations), a real tint measurably brightens the isolated specular albedo (0.42282 -> 0.42514), and reciprocity survives (f82Tint depends only on the shared half-vector angle cosM, exactly as schlick(cosM,f0) already did). The diffuse-coupling term deliberately does NOT read edgeTint -- a different physical approximation, not this round's target. STILL NOT CLAIMED: fidelity of mu=1/7 and n=6 to a REAL metal's measured Fresnel curve (the peak-at-the-pin finding shows the two are a matched pair WITHIN this bump-shape family, not that this family is what a real metal's silhouette dip looks like -- that needs tabulated complex-IOR data, which is portsmouth/F82-tint-generator's own job, still unread); an F90 parameter (inherits schlick()'s own hardcoded F90=1); and no device-side material reads fresnelF82Wgsl.mjs yet -- principled.mjs's own wiring runs the CPU function only.",
