        note: "DONE at v4586, SCOPED rather than the full port this entry described. What shipped is not gkjohnson/three-edge-projection or clipper2-js -- it is fx/spritemesh/blueprint.js's own occlusion test rebuilt as a TRUE 3D line-of-sight query against mesh/meshBVH.mjs (already in this tree, already trusted), which delivers the entry's actual practical want -- correct hidden-line removal, including edges that are only PARTIALLY occluded -- for blueprint.js's real use case (one mesh, one viewpoint) without pulling in a second dependency (clipper2-js) whose only job is merging SILHOUETTES ACROSS MULTIPLE OBJECTS, which blueprint.html's single-ship view never needed.\n\nTHE OLD CODE: one sample (an edge's midpoint), compared to front-facing triangles' 2D screen-space depth at that XY, whole edge marked hidden or not as one unit -- O(edges * faces), no acceleration structure. THE NEW CODE: the projection's own implied pinhole eye (proven, not assumed -- parametrising the ray from E=(0,0,persp) through a view-space point and solving for where it crosses the screen plane reproduces rotProject's own `persp/(persp-Z)` exactly, so the mesh's already-rotated vertices feed the BVH with no separate camera) casts a real MeshBVH.intersectsSegment visibility ray to 9 points along each kept edge (default; opts.samples) and walks the result into maximal same-visibility runs, so a half-occluded crease comes back as two segments, not one guess. No front-face filter, unlike the old code -- a true segment test needs none, ANYTHING between eye and point blocks the view. Self-grazing at the sample point (every sample sits exactly on the mesh, being a point on one of its own edges) is handled by intersectsSegment's own t>eps && t<1-eps cutoff, not worked around separately.\n\nHAND-VERIFIED, NOT JUST PLAUSIBLE: a synthetic scene (a flat panel's top edge, X from -1 to 1 at Y=1; a second, closer panel positioned from the projection formula itself to cover exactly X<=-0.4) gets exactly 2 segments back, split at exactly the worked-out X boundary. The OLD algorithm was then actually RUN (not merely reasoned about) on that identical scene: its one sample sits at X=0, outside the occluder's footprint, so it reports the WHOLE edge visible -- missing a real 25% occlusion entirely. That is the exact, reproduced bug this round fixes, not a hypothetical one.\n\nTHE OCCLUSION PRIMITIVE ITSELF IS PROVEN, NOT SPOT-CHECKED: for a convex solid, a point on a face whose outward normal points toward the eye is visible and a point facing away is not (a line from outside a convex body crosses its boundary at most twice, and a toward-the-eye point is the first crossing) -- checked against MeshBVH.intersectsSegment on a box's 12 triangle interiors across 4 non-axis-aligned angles, 0 contradictions. *** ONE HONEST WRINKLE FOUND ALONG THE WAY, LEFT ALONE ON PURPOSE: *** blueprint.js's own faceFacing (used for silhouette/crease classification, unchanged by this round) is a cheaper orthographic-style stand-in -- just the sign of the normal's Z-component -- that is only exactly the true perspective test for a camera looking straight down -Z. Measured disagreeing with the exact test at a large oblique angle (ry=1.9, rx=0.8: faceFacing says front-facing, the true dot(normal, EYE-point) says facing away). Pre-existing, not touched by the occlusion rewrite, and out of this round's scope -- the gate proves the NEW occlusion primitive against the exact test, not against the neighbouring approximation.\n\nMEASURED, NOT ASSUMED: on the demo's own mesh shape (spriteMesh.js's extrude() topology at its real segments:48, 192 triangles), the new approach costs 2.6-2.8ms/call against the old one's ~1.3ms -- roughly 2x, a real and expected cost (a BVH build plus 9 rays per kept edge instead of one depth compare) -- and still comfortable under a 60fps frame budget with headroom to spare. Confirmed visually too: headless Chromium screenshots of the actual blueprint.html, two different procedurally-shelled ships, both the solid line-art view and the dashed-hidden-line 'ghost' toggle, show clean occlusion-correct outlines with no stray interior clutter and no missing silhouette segments, zero page errors.\n\nPermanent gate: fx/spritemesh/blueprint-selfcheck.mjs (6 sections -- structural shape, the convex-solid proof above, the hand-verified partial-occlusion scene, the old algorithm run for real on that same scene, an unoccluded-triangle regression check, and the measured performance comparison). Confirmed to have teeth: sabotaging the new occlusion call to always report 'visible' turns section 3 red, everything else stays green (correctly narrow -- the convex-solid proof exercises meshBVH.mjs directly, not blueprint()'s use of it). STILL NOT CLAIMED: no multi-object silhouette merge (the clipper2-js half of the original entry) -- nothing needs it yet, blueprint.html draws one ship at a time; three-edge-projection itself was not ported, read, or vendored; blueprint.html did not need to change and was not touched, only screenshotted for confirmation; and the faceFacing approximation named above is a real, pre-existing imprecision left exactly as found.",
