        what: "This tree maintains a family of 'measure the real device capability rather than assume it' probes (render/threeProbe.mjs, ui/webgpuProbe.mjs, ui/codecProbe.mjs) but has none for shader float/int/uint PRECISION specifically -- grepped for a precision calculator and found nothing. This gap is not hypothetical: this session alone closed out two separate precision-divergence bugs (bcs_hash's sin/fract hash diverging up to 0.68 between f32/f64, tools/ship/noisePrecision-selfcheck.mjs's Ashima-noise decimal-truncation mismatch) and the swizzle wall is a THIRD device-capability surprise, of a different kind, from the same general category: what this box's GPU/browser actually does, versus what the spec or a naive assumption says it does.",
        how: "gkjohnson/webgl-precision (MIT) measures precision EMPIRICALLY rather than reading it off a spec: for floats, progressively adds shrinking negative powers of 2 to 1.0 until they stop having an effect; for int/uint, bit-shifts until the value rolls over or goes negative. Documented against ARM's own mobile-GPU precision benchmarking articles. Small enough to hand-adapt into a precisionProbe.mjs sibling to the existing probe family, following the same 'pure, every global handed in as an argument' convention ui/codecProbe.mjs already documents in its own header, rather than adding it as a page-only tool.",
        why: "This tree has now hit real, load-bearing precision divergences three separate times without a standing instrument for the underlying question ('what does THIS device's shader precision actually do') -- each was diagnosed after the fact, from its own specific symptom. A probe would not have predicted bcs_hash's sin-hash problem (that is an algorithmic choice, not a precision-availability question) but it is squarely the right instrument for future noise/hash/precision work to consult before guessing.",
